  "output": "Now I have a clear picture. Here's the full performance analysis:\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\nThis is a Python/FastAPI backend — no React. I'll address the five categories against what actually exists.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 Query Pattern — `save_policy_triples` (Critical)\n\n**`src/data/db.py:467-501`** — For each triple in the batch, the loop executes **4 separate DB operations**: one `INSERT` for the triple, two `_upsert_node` calls (subject + object), and one edge `INSERT`. For N triples that's 4N round-trips, all within a single connection but still N sequential awaits.\n\n```python\n# CURRENT — O(4N) sequential awaits\nasync def save_policy_triples(self, triples: list[PolicyTriple]) -> None:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:\n        for triple in triples:\n            await db.execute(...)          # 1 per triple\n            subject_node_id = await self._upsert_node(...)  # 2 per triple\n            object_node_id  = await self._upsert_node(...)\n            await db.execute(...)          # edge insert\n        await db.commit()\n```\n\n**Fix — use `executemany` and pre-compute node IDs:**\n\n```python\nasync def save_policy_triples(self, triples: list[PolicyTriple]) -> None:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:\n        # Batch insert all triples\n        await db.executemany(\n            \"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO policy_triples (...) VALUES (...)\",\n            [self._triple_params(t) for t in triples],\n        )\n\n        # Deduplicate nodes, then batch upsert\n        node_params = {}\n        for t in triples:\n            for text in (t.subject, t.object_):\n                node_id = hashlib.sha256(text.lower().encode()).hexdigest()\n                node_params[node_id] = (node_id, text, None, t.source_url)\n        await db.executemany(\n            \"\"\"INSERT INTO graph_nodes (node_id, entity_text, entity_type, first_seen_url)\n               VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\n               ON CONFLICT(entity_text) DO UPDATE SET mention_count = mention_count + 1\"\"\",\n            list(node_params.values()),\n        )\n\n        # Batch insert edges\n        def _node_id(text: str) -> str:\n            return hashlib.sha256(text.lower().encode()).hexdigest()\n\n        await db.executemany(\n            \"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO graph_edges (...) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n            [\n                (\n                    f\"{t.triple_id}:edge\",\n                    _node_id(t.subject),\n                    t.predicate_lemma,\n                    _node_id(t.object_),\n                    t.triple_id,\n                    2.0 if \"CROSS_INSTRUMENT_CORROBORATION\" in t.validation_flags else 1.0,\n                )\n                for t in triples\n            ],\n        )\n        await db.commit()\n```\n\nThis reduces 4N awaits to 3 `executemany` calls regardless of N.\n\n---\n\n### 2. New DB Connection Per Operation (High)\n\n**`src/data/db.py` — every method does `async with aiosqlite.connect(...)`**. Each call opens, authenticates, and closes the SQLite file handle. With 8 specialists each saving responses, this fires ~40 connect/close cycles per query.\n\n**Fix — persistent connection with WAL mode:**\n\n```python\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path: Path = _DB_PATH) -> None:\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._conn: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n\n    async def _get_conn(self) -> aiosqlite.Connection:\n        if self._conn is None:\n            self._conn = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n            self._conn.row_factory = aiosqlite.Row\n            await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n            await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL\")\n        return self._conn\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._conn:\n            await self._conn.close()\n            self._conn = None\n```\n\nPair with WAL mode — it allows concurrent reads during writes, which matters during parallel specialist fetches.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Re-parsing on Every Cache Hit (High)\n\n**`src/fetch/engine.py:97-101`** — `_schedule_deterministic_parsing` is called on every cache hit, unconditionally:\n\n```python\ncached = await self._cache.get(url)\nif cached is not None:\n    self._schedule_deterministic_parsing(url, cached.content)  # ALWAYS fires\n    return cached.content\n```\n\n`db.py` already has `is_document_parsed(source_url)` at line 548, but it's never called in this path. This means every time a specialist reads a cached page, it fires a background NLP parse task — even if the document was parsed yesterday.\n\n**Fix:**\n\n```python\nasync def _do_fetch(self, url: str) -> str:\n    cached = await self._cache.get(url)\n    if cached is not None:\n        logger.debug(\"Cache hit for %s\", url)\n        # Only schedule parse if document hasn't been processed yet\n        if not await self._db.is_document_parsed(url):\n            self._schedule_deterministic_parsing(url, cached.content)\n        return cached.content\n    ...\n```\n\nThis also requires injecting `DatabaseManager` into `FetchEngine`, which is already used by `FetchCache` — pass it through rather than creating a new instance.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Untracked Fire-and-Forget Tasks (Memory Leak Risk)\n\n**`src/fetch/engine.py:185-198`** — `_schedule_deterministic_parsing` calls `asyncio.create_task(run_parser())` with no reference stored. Tasks are garbage-collected if the task object is dropped, but more importantly there's no backpressure. If 8 specialists each fetch 4 docs simultaneously, 32 NLP tasks can queue up, each loading a spaCy pipeline.\n\n**Fix — track tasks and apply a semaphore:**\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, ...) -> None:\n    ...\n    self._parse_semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(2)  # max 2 concurrent parses\n    self._active_parse_tasks: set[asyncio.Task] = set()\n\ndef _schedule_deterministic_parsing(self, url: str, content: str) -> None:\n    if not self._config.parsing.enabled:\n        return\n\n    async def run_parser() -> None:\n        async with self._parse_semaphore:\n            try:\n                await get_sage_pipeline().process_document_for_url(url, content)\n            except Exception as e:\n                logger.warning(\"Deterministic parsing failed for %s: %s\", url, e)\n\n    try:\n        task = asyncio.create_task(run_parser())\n        self._active_parse_tasks.add(task)\n        task.add_done_callback(self._active_parse_tasks.discard)\n    except RuntimeError:\n        pass\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Per-Domain Lock is Global (Medium)\n\n**`src/fetch/engine.py:84-89`** — The rate-limit delay check uses a single `asyncio.Lock` across all domains:\n\n```python\nasync with self._domain_lock:   # ALL domains block here\n    last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n    delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n    if delay_required > 0:\n        await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)  # entire system pauses for 1 domain\n```\n\nIf the `tbs-sct` domain needs a 0.8s delay, requests to `laws-lois.justice.gc.ca` (a different domain) are unnecessarily blocked.\n\n**Fix — per-domain locks:**\n\n```python\nself._domain_locks: dict[str, asyncio.Lock] = defaultdict(asyncio.Lock)\n\nasync def _fetch_with_concurrency(self, url: str) -> str:\n    domain = self._get_domain(url)\n    async with self._domain_locks[domain]:   # only blocks same-domain requests\n        last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n        delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n        if delay_required > 0:\n            await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)\n        self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.time()\n    async with self._domain_semaphores[domain]:\n        return await self._do_fetch(url)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. Regex Recompiled on Every `fetch()` Call (Low-Medium)\n\n**`src/fetch/engine.py:74-77`** — The `_normalize_url` method re-imports `re` and calls `re.search` with a raw string pattern on every invocation:\n\n```python\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    import re\n    if re.search(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$', url):\n        ...\n```\n\n**Fix — module-level compiled pattern:**\n\n```python\nimport re\n_LAWS_ACT_RE = re.compile(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$')\n\n# in method:\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    if _LAWS_ACT_RE.search(url):\n        return url.rstrip('/') + '/FullText.html'\n    return url\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. Missing Index on `agent_responses.query_id`\n\n**`src/data/db.py:_SCHEMA`** — `get_agent_responses` filters by `query_id` (line 249) but the schema has no index on `agent_responses(query_id)`. As the table grows (one row per agent per query), this becomes a full scan.\n\n**Fix — add to `_SCHEMA`:**\n\n```python\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_agent_responses_query ON agent_responses(query_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_orch_responses_query ON orchestrator_responses(query_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_feedback_query ON feedback(query_id);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 8. `db.initialize()` Inside Teacher Loop (Low)\n\n**`src/agents/specialist.py:190`** — `_save_teacher_notes` calls `await self._db.initialize()` every invocation, which re-runs the full `executescript` with all `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` statements even though the DB is already set up.\n\n**Fix** — call `initialize()` once at app startup (already done in `src/main.py` presumably) and remove the call from `_save_teacher_notes`.\n\n---\n\n### 9. `get_coverage_stats` — 4 Separate COUNT Queries (Low)\n\n**`src/data/db.py:557-568`** — Four sequential `COUNT(*)` queries when one will do:\n\n```python\nasync def get_coverage_stats(self) -> dict:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:\n        async with db.execute(\"\"\"\n            SELECT\n                (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM kiss_documents) AS documents,\n                (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM policy_triples)  AS triples,\n                (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM graph_nodes)     AS nodes,\n                (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM graph_edges)     AS edges\n        \"\"\") as cursor:\n            row = await cursor.fetchone()\n    return {\"documents\": row[0], \"triples\": row[1], \"nodes\": row[2], \"edges\": row[3]}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Issue | Location | Impact | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| N+1 in `save_policy_triples` | `db.py:467` | Critical — 4N DB ops → 3 | `executemany` batching |\n| Connection per operation | `db.py` all methods | High — 40+ connect/close per query | Persistent connection + WAL |\n| Re-parse on cache hit | `engine.py:100` | High — redundant NLP on every cache read | Check `is_document_parsed` first |\n| Untracked parse tasks | `engine.py:195` | Medium — unbounded task queue | Semaphore + task set |\n| Global domain lock | `engine.py:84` | Medium — serializes all domains | Per-domain locks |\n| Regex recompiled per call | `engine.py:74` | Low — compile at module level | `_LAWS_ACT_RE` constant |\n| Missing FK indexes | `db.py:_SCHEMA` | Medium — grows with usage | Add 3 index lines |\n| `initialize()` in teacher loop | `specialist.py:190` | Low — wasted DDL execution | Remove, call once at startup |\n| 4 COUNT queries in stats | `db.py:558` | Low | Merge into one query |\n",
